HYPOTHESIS (associational, not causal). Increasing government education expenditure is associated with
smaller per-capita-income gains in Sub-Saharan Africa than in the broader lower-middle-income aggregate
it is largely nested in - and in neither aggregate is there a positive long-run association strong enough
to call a return. Specifically, in this WDI panel (GNI per capita, constant 2015 US$; education
expenditure as % of GNI):

(1) Levels/trend band (1975-2015, LMC N=41): the trend-adjusted association is NEGATIVE and significant,
b = -0.20 ln points per +1pp of GNI devoted to education (NW SE 0.07, p=0.002; -0.21 and -0.18 for lags 1
and 3, all p<0.005; detrended r = -0.55). This negative coefficient should not be read as a penalty for
spending: education's share of GNI declined as income trended upward, income sits in the denominator of
the treatment, and the aggregate series share a common trend.

(2) Changes band (LMC N=40): the sign FLIPS - dln(GNI/cap) ~ d(education share) gives +0.022 (SE 0.010,
p=0.035), or +0.040 (SE 0.011, p<0.001) with a trend, i.e. roughly +2 to +4% income growth per +1pp of
expenditure share. This positive co-movement is fragile: restricted to 1991-2015 (N=25) the same
differenced coefficient is -0.031 (p=0.48), and in levels most of the negative coefficient is absorbed by
gross enrolment and labour-force controls (from -0.98 to -0.05, p=0.52).

(3) Sub-Saharan Africa: spending was persistently HIGHER (mean 4.30 vs 3.50% of GNI; peak 6.10% in 1997
vs 3.87% in 1999) and fell after 2000 in both groups. But SSA income is observed for 2011-2015 only (n=5),
so its slope (-0.10 vs -0.45 for LMC in levels; implied interaction +0.35, p=0.24, N=46) is descriptive,
not an estimate, and the SSA/LMC overlap attenuates any true difference toward zero.

(4) In the only comparable window (2011-2015) SSA spent more (mean 3.41 vs 3.16% of GNI) yet grew far less
(+5.1% vs +13.8%), its year-to-year co-movement with spending was negative (r(dlnY,d edu) = -0.47), and its
income level (1634 US$ in 2015) equalled the lower-middle-income aggregate's 2009 level, with the gap
widening from 171 to 331 US$.

(5) Mechanism: education expenditure co-moves NEGATIVELY with enrolment in changes in LMC
(secondary dGER ~ deduc: -0.79, p=0.007) and shows NO detectable payoff in SSA (secondary -0.26, p=0.21;
primary +0.25, p=0.59), even though SSA achieved a large access expansion (primary GER 60.6->97.0,
secondary 14.8->43.4, still ~21pp below LMC in 2015). Within LMC, the decade with the highest expenditure
share (1990s, 3.71%) was not the decade with the highest income growth (2000s, +39%).

Synthesis: the marginal income gain associated with an additional percentage point of GNI directed to
education is small, positive only in year-to-year changes, and statistically undetectable for
Sub-Saharan Africa, which sustained the highest expenditure shares in the panel while converting them
into school access rather than into measurable per-capita income gains - the income gap to the broader
developing-country aggregate widened rather than narrowed. Stronger conclusions require country-level data
and an identification strategy; none is available from two group aggregates with 5 SSA income
observations.

RESUMEN EN ESPAÑOL (misma hipótesis, alcance correlacional): el aumento del gasto en educación (como % del
ingreso nacional) se asocia con ganancias de ingreso per cápita MENORES en África subsahariana que en el
agregado de países de ingreso medio-bajo en el que está mayormente contenido; y en ninguno de los dos
agregados la asociación de largo plazo es positiva. En LMC (N=41, 1975-2015) la asociación en niveles
corregida por tendencia es NEGATIVA (-0.20 puntos log por +1pp, p=0.002), mientras que en variaciones
anuales el signo se INVIERTE (+0.022, p=0.035; +0.040 con tendencia, p<0.001; ~+2 a +4% de crecimiento por
+1pp), resultado que desaparece al restringir la muestra a 1991-2015. SSA gastó sistemáticamente más
(4.30% vs 3.50% del RNB, pico 6.10% en 1997) pero solo tiene ingreso observado en 2011-2015 (n=5), con lo
que su pendiente (-0.10; interacción +0.35, p=0.24, N=46) es descriptiva, no una estimación, y el
solapamiento SSA/LMC atenúa cualquier diferencia hacia cero. En la única ventana comparable (2011-2015) SSA
gastó más y creció menos (+5.1% vs +13.8%) y su brecha de ingreso con LMC se amplió de 171 a 331 US$, con su
nivel de 2015 igual al de LMC en 2009. El mecanismo es de acceso, no de ingreso: matrícula primaria SSA
60.6->97.0 y secundaria 14.8->43.4 (aún ~21pp bajo LMC), sin efecto detectable del gasto sobre la matrícula
en diferencias. Ninguna afirmación causal es sostenible con dos agregados y 5 observaciones de ingreso en SSA.
